WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.896
In this tutorial, we will rotate raster data back through time in GPlates, and use an age grid to make it smoother.

2
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.611
A raster is an image made of pixels, like a JPEG or a netCDF file.

3
00:00:15.611 --> 00:00:21.244
It is different from vector data, which is points, lines and polygons.

4
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:28.813
You can download the files for this exercise from the Tutorial 3.

5
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:29.364
2 link.

6
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.252
Exercise 1: rotating and cutting a raster.

7
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:44.232
Go to File, then Open Feature Collection, and open the rotation file and the static polygons file.

8
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:52.788
Now go to File, then Import, then Import Raster, and select the topography image.

9
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:02.112
In the Layers window, keep the raster at the bottom and the polygons on top.

10
00:01:02.112 --> 00:01:04.100
Drag the layers if needed.

11
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:11.268
Expand the raster layer by clicking the small triangle on its left.

12
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:21.146
In Inputs, under Reconstructed Polygons, click Add new connection, and select the static polygons.

13
00:01:21.146 --> 00:01:23.788
Give GPlates a moment to process.

14
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.667
Now move the time slider back, for example to 50 Ma.

15
00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:37.716
The raster is cut into plates and rotated to its past position.

16
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.508
To see only the raster, hide the polygons by clicking their eye icon.

17
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.090
Notice the big grey gaps along the mid-ocean ridges.

18
00:01:54.090 --> 00:02:00.212
Whole polygons disappear at once, so the seafloor jumps instead of growing smoothly.

19
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.609
Exercise 2: age-based masking.

20
00:02:07.609 --> 00:02:12.124
We will fix those gaps with the seafloor age grid.

21
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.028
Import the age grid the same way.

22
00:02:18.028 --> 00:02:23.707
If GPlates asks whether to use the existing GPML file, click No.

23
00:02:23.707 --> 00:02:26.812
Then choose age as the raster band.

24
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:38.683
The static polygons should still be connected to the topography layer, under Reconstructed Polygons.

25
00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:46.900
Now, in the topography layer, under Inputs, find Age grid raster, click Add new connection, and select the age grid.

26
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.000
Make sure you add it to the topography layer, not to the age grid layer.

27
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:04.640
As we move back in time, the seafloor progressively disappears based on its age, producing a smoother reconstruction.

28
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:15.888
This works because every pixel in the age grid has its own age, instead of one age for a whole polygon.

29
00:03:15.888 --> 00:03:23.376
For example, at 50 Ma, seafloor younger than 50 million years is hidden, because it had not formed yet.

30
00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:27.574
That's it.

31
00:03:27.574 --> 00:03:34.216
You rotated a raster through time, and used an age grid to mask it smoothly.

32
00:03:34.216 --> 00:03:35.964
Thanks for watching.
